


     GRAND SYNTHESIS: THE TANH-MERSENNE-TOURNAMENT CONNECTION

                     opus-2026-03-16-S90au


A chain of exact identities discovered in this session, each
flowing inevitably from the previous:


LAYER 1: THE CAYLEY TRANSFORM
==============================

    Q(x) = (1+x)/(1-x)       maps (-1,1) to (0,inf)
    Q^{-1}(n) = (n-1)/(n+1)  the "Cayley address" of n

    arctanh(x) = (1/2) ln Q(x)  the rapidity
    tanh(w) = Q^{-1}(e^{2w})    the inverse


LAYER 2: THE FORMAL GROUP LAW
==============================

    F(x,y) = (x+y)/(1+xy)     the tanh addition law

    F(x_m, x_n) = x_{mn}       ON CAYLEY ADDRESSES, F = MULTIPLICATION

    Q: ((-1,1), F) -> ((0,inf), *)   the Cayley transform is a
                                       GROUP HOMOMORPHISM

    [m](x) = tanh(m*arctanh(x))     the m-fold series: EXPONENTIATION


LAYER 3: POWERS OF 2 ON THE CAYLEY LINE
=========================================

    x_{2^k} = (2^k - 1)/(2^k + 1)        Cayley address of 2^k
    arctanh(x_{2^k}) = k * ln(2)/2       at rapidity k/2 bits
    tanh(k * ln(2)/2) = (2^k-1)/(2^k+1)  equally spaced at 1/2 bit

    Numerator: 2^k - 1 = MERSENNE NUMBER
    Denominator: 2^k + 1 = FERMAT-LIKE NUMBER

    These are the LATTICE POINTS of the Cayley line in half-bits.


LAYER 4: THE k-NACCI TRANSFER MATRIX
======================================

    M_k = k x k companion matrix with first row all 1's
    Char poly: p(x) = x^k - x^{k-1} - ... - x - 1
    ALL coefficients = -1 (the DEFINING PROPERTY)

    Augmented: (x-1)*p(x) = x^{k+1} - 2x^k + 1
    Dominant eigenvalue: tau_k -> 2 as k -> inf


LAYER 5: THM-227 (THE k-NACCI MERSENNE IDENTITY)
==================================================

    Tr(M_k^n) = 2^n - 1    for all 1 <= n <= k

    PROVED by induction on Newton's identities:
      t(n) = sum_{m=1}^{n-1} t(m) + n  =>  t(n) = 2^n - 1

    INDEPENDENT of k (all k-nacci matrices agree in the "Newton range")

    At n = k+1: Tr(M_k^{k+1}) = 2^{k+1} - k - 2 (deficit = k+1)

    The TRANSITION at n = k is a phase transition:
      Growth rate drops from 2 to tau_k.
      The system "discovers its dimension."


LAYER 6: THE DOMINANT EIGENVALUE IDENTITY
==========================================

    tau_k^k = 1/(2 - tau_k)

    From the augmented poly: x^{k+1} - 2x^k + 1 = 0
    Divide by x^k: x - 2 + x^{-k} = 0
    Therefore: x^k = 1/(2-x)

    At k=2: phi^2 = 1/(2-phi) = phi + 1
    (The golden ratio identity is the k=2 case!)


LAYER 7: THE WITT VECTOR INTERPRETATION
=========================================

    Ghost map of the big Witt ring:
      w_n(a) = sum_{d|n} d * a_d^{n/d}

    Teichmuller lift [2]: ghost = 2^n, coordinates = M(n,2)
    Teichmuller lift [1]: ghost = 1, coordinates = delta_{n,1}

    The MERSENNE Witt vector [2] - [1]:
      ghost_n = 2^n - 1

    This IS Tr(M_k^n) for the k-nacci matrix (n <= k)!

    The k-nacci trace sequence = ghost components of [2]-[1].


LAYER 8: THE NECKLACE-ORBIT CONNECTION
=======================================

    M(n,2) = necklace polynomial = binary Lyndon words of length n
    sum_{d|n} d * M(d,2) = 2^n  (orbit counting)
    2^n - 1 = (all binary orbits) - (fixed point)

    The k-nacci trace COUNTS non-trivial binary periodic orbits.
    The fixed point (eigenvalue 1) is removed by the augmented poly.


LAYER 9: THE FORBIDDEN VALUES
===============================

    H = 7  = 2^3 - 1 = Tr(M_3^3) = the tribonacci forbidden value
    H = 21 = 3 * 7 = related but NOT a Mersenne number

    At odd prime k:
      Tr(M_k^k) = 2^k - 1 is the k-nacci forbidden value
      When 2^k-1 is prime: Mersenne prime = forbidden AND prime

    The forbidden values live at the LATTICE POINTS of the Cayley line:
      H = 7 at 3/2 bits, H = 31 at 5/2 bits, H = 127 at 7/2 bits, ...


LAYER 10: THE MULTIPLICATIVE ORDER
====================================

    Tr(M_k^n) = 2^n - 1 = 0 mod p  iff  ord_p(2) | n

    p = 7: ord_7(2) = 3, so 2^3 - 1 = 7 = 0 mod 7
    p = 31: ord_31(2) = 5, so 2^5 - 1 = 31 = 0 mod 31
    p = 127: ord_127(2) = 7, so 2^7 - 1 = 127 = 0 mod 127

    For Mersenne primes p = 2^k - 1:
      ord_p(2) = k (since 2^k = 1 mod p by definition)

    The forbidden values are the FIRST multiples of Mersenne primes
    in the trace sequence.


THE THREAD:

  tanh -> formal group -> multiplication -> powers of 2 -> Mersenne numbers
  -> k-nacci traces -> Newton's identities -> all-(-1) coefficients
  -> augmented polynomial -> Witt vectors -> necklace polynomials
  -> periodic orbits -> forbidden H values -> back to tournaments

  Everything is connected by the formal group law of tournament composition.
  Everything follows from the number 2 (the tournament base).
  Everything is encoded in the Cayley transform Q(x) = (1+x)/(1-x).


THE IDENTITIES (exact, proved):

  1. tanh(ln(n)/2) = (n-1)/(n+1)             [Cayley-tanh]
  2. F(x_m, x_n) = x_{mn}                     [formal group = multiplication]
  3. Q(F(x,y)) = Q(x) * Q(y)                  [Q is a homomorphism]
  4. Tr(M_k^n) = 2^n - 1 for 1 <= n <= k      [THM-227]
  5. tau_k^k = 1/(2 - tau_k)                   [augmented polynomial]
  6. ghost_n([2]-[1]) = 2^n - 1                [Witt]
  7. Tr(M_k^{k+1}) = 2^{k+1} - k - 2          [first departure]

  Plus: phi^2 = phi + 1 is identity (5) at k = 2.


